<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mahaut, Countess of Artois</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1268–1329</span> · Also known as Mahaut of Artois</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mahaut was countess of Artois from 1302, after her father Robert II was killed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-golden-spurs">Battle of the Golden Spurs</a>. Her nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-iii-of-artois">Robert III of Artois</a> repeatedly challenged her inheritance, but royal courts upheld her claim. She was an energetic ruler and patron of the arts, and her daughters Joan and Blanche married sons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-iv-of-france">Philip IV of France</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mahaut-countess-of-artois.jpg" alt="Mahaut, Countess of Artois" width="170" height="234" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Antonio de Succa · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mahaut.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
